<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Los Altos</h1><p class="st-meta">1838 CE – 1840 CE · capital Quetzaltenango</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Breakaway western highlands of Guatemala, recognised briefly as the sixth state of the Central American Federation before Rafael Carrera crushed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ladino and criollo elites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango">Quetzaltenango</a> and the western highlands had long resented domination by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. In February 1838, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera's</a> revolt shook <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a>, they declared a separate state, and the federal congress recognised it in June 1838 as the sixth state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Los Altos was a liberal government led by men such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcelo-molina">Marcelo Molina</a>, ruling a region with a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiche-people">K'iche'</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mam-people">Mam Maya</a> majority who had little say. It imposed taxes and levies that alienated indigenous communities, many of whom sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Carrera</a>. The state depended on the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> and the federal army.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Morazán</a>'s defeat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Carrera</a> invaded in January 1840, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango">Quetzaltenango</a> and executed leading liberals, reincorporating the territory into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-guatemala">State of Guatemala</a>. A brief revival in 1848 also failed. The quetzal-bird emblem of Los Altos later appeared on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala's</a> flag.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-los-altos.jpg" alt="State of Los Altos" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_State_of_Los_Altos.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1838 CE – 1840 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Quetzaltenango</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Marcelo Molina</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, K'iche' and Mam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Los Altos, Sixth State of Los Altos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1839&amp;focus=state-of-los-altos" aria-label="State of Los Altos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2178 1626 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Los Altos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2498 1882L2525 1889M2498 1882L2525 1889"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2525 1889h0M2525 1889h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2498 1882h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2498 1882h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Quetzaltenango. <a href="/atlas?year=1839&amp;focus=state-of-los-altos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
